    "answer": {
      "short": "Proved: $a_1a_2\\cdots a_n=(\\frac{S_n}{R_n})^{n/2}$.\n\nUse the finite geometric-sum formula for the terms and their reciprocals to obtain $\\frac{S_n}{R_n}=a^2r^{n-1}$.",
      "worked": "Let the first term be $a>0$ and the common ratio be $r>0$. For $r\\ne1$, the reciprocals have first term $\\frac{1}{a}$ and common ratio $\\frac{1}{r}$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}S_n&=\\frac{a(r^n-1)}{r-1}\\\\R_n&=\\frac{\\frac1a(r^{-n}-1)}{r^{-1}-1}\\\\R_n&=\\frac{r^n-1}{ar^{n-1}(r-1)}\\\\\\frac{S_n}{R_n}&=a^2r^{n-1}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe product of the terms is\n$$\\begin{align*}a_1a_2\\cdots a_n&=a^nr^{0+1+\\cdots+(n-1)}\\\\a_1a_2\\cdots a_n&=a^nr^{n(n-1)/2}\\\\a_1a_2\\cdots a_n&=(a^2r^{n-1})^{n/2}\\\\a_1a_2\\cdots a_n&=\\left(\\frac{S_n}{R_n}\\right)^{n/2}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nIf $r=1$, all terms equal $a$, so $S_n=na$ and $R_n=\\frac{n}{a}$. Hence $(\\frac{S_n}{R_n})^{n/2}=(a^2)^{n/2}=a^n=a_1a_2\\cdots a_n$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 100
  },
